    "summary": "Soaring Jerry solo, high‑energy Shakedown Street, solid set despite PA hiccups",
    "blurb": "Jerry delivers a soaring solo on Shakedown Street and drives the second‑set energy. The band rides strong through Cassidy, Jack Straw and a spirited Good Lovin' encore. PA failures toward the end slightly mar the listening experience.",
    "review": "January 7, 1979 marked the Dead’s inaugural Madison Square Garden gig, and the show lives up to its historic weight with plenty of fireworks. The second set erupts with an electrifying Shakedown Street that showcases a spectacular Jerry Garcia solo—often cited as one of the night’s high points—and keeps the momentum rolling through Cassidy and Jack Straw. Good Lovin' closes the night on a feel‑good note, proving the band could still summon that classic Dead joy even in a massive arena.\nHowever, listeners should be aware that both audience recordings capture recurring PA problems that bite into the final three songs, most noticeably during Black Peter where feedback cuts through the mix. While the tape’s ambience retains the raw excitement of MSG, occasional clarity issues mean the latter half feels a bit compromised compared to the earlier fire‑starter tracks.",
